  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led and still had a chance. Arizona took it 5\u20134 in the ninth when it mattered, and the Giants fell back a full game in a division that doesn't forgive one-run losses. This is what fragile looks like from the inside.",
    "what_this_means": "Mahle lasted 4.1 innings before the arm gave out, and by then the Diamondbacks had already struck in the fifth. Sam Hentges and JT Brubaker bridged the gap without bleeding more damage. Brubaker threw 3.1 clean. But the offense was never ahead to protect. They scraped together nine hits and converted three runners in scoring position into runs, which should have been enough. It wasn't. They chased from inning one and caught up in the ninth with two runs, but Arizona had already decided the game. This is what chasing feels like: real contact, genuine chances, zero margin for error. The bullpen didn't fail. The lineup did just enough but not enough. Inconsistency isn't a mood anymore. It's a fact embedded in how they win and lose.",
    "what_to_watch": "AZ sends a TBD starter tonight. The Giants' window in the second half starts now. Landen Roupp gets the ball.",
